# Files and directories the stack owns

TODO. For each path below: what it is, why the operator owns it, what
happens if you edit it by hand.

Short answer: if a file has an `ansible_managed` header, the operator's
next `./catena site` run will overwrite your edits. Don't hand-edit these:

- `/etc/dokploy/traefik/dynamic/*.yml` — managed by `dashboard-sync`
- `/etc/catena/backup.env` — managed by Ansible role
- `/etc/catena/restic.pass` — managed by Ansible role
- `/etc/systemd/system/catena-*.{service,timer}` — managed by Ansible
- `/usr/local/bin/run-backup.sh`, `/usr/local/bin/dashboard-sync`,
  `/usr/local/bin/gatus-sync`, `/usr/local/bin/auto-update.sh` — managed
  by Ansible

What you CAN touch without asking:

- Apps deployed through Dokploy's UI (those are yours)
- Your Keycloak users/groups (that's the point of self-service SSO)
- Anything in `/home/<your-users>/` (the operator doesn't touch home dirs)
